  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION FOR 00Z TAF CYCLE....\n\nSanta Ana winds at this lunch hour continue to be fairly weak and\nconfined to coastal foothills and adjacent valleys. This is due \nto an area of low pressure that is beginning to form over the \nGreat Basin, which will continue to push to the southeast over \nArizona this weekend, becoming a closed low. Northerly and \neasterly winds on the backside of this system will contribute to \nbreezy winds in typical areas of the coastal foothills and valleys\nthrough the weekend. The winds currently in a northerly direction\nwill begin to shift more from the northeast on Saturday, favoring\ngreater winds in San Diego County. Ensemble models generally \npoint to winds gusting locally near 30-45 MPH Saturday morning and\nafternoon. Fire weather conditions will be fairly lower end due \nto recent rains and modest overnight recoveries. \n\nThis weather pattern will be conducive for very warm temperatures\nwest of the mountains to the coast by this weekend. Confidence is \nhigh in seeing temperatures in the mid to upper 80s. The warmest \nareas will be on Sunday near the coast into the far western valleys \nof Orange and San Diego Counties. Some inland valleys may reach \ninto the lower 90s with these areas seeing about a 35-65% chance of \nreaching over 90 degrees. Heat risk will be moderate for a short time \nin the early afternoon hours, so keep this in mind if attending \noutdoor events or working outside.\n\nWarmer than normal temperatures look to stick around as model \nguidance shows an area of high pressure maintaining its strength \nover the region into the middle of next week. This will keep \ntemperatures around 5-10 degrees above normal area wide. The \nforecast becomes more uncertain by the latter half of next week as a \ntroughing pattern becomes more apparent over the NE Pacific. Details \non where and when this system nears the area is still in question. \nThere remains better confidence in temperatures cooling back closer \nto normal by next weekend, but we continue to look into if this \npattern will bring us any rain. For now, dry weather will occur \nthrough at least next Thursday.",
